{"w":2400,"h":3106,"lines":[{"t":"Test 4","c":1,"x":0.15583432470650863,"h":0.015393524882260645,"y":0.062070678939974311,"w":0.056752401987711587},{"t":"READING","x":0.47368421263127913,"y":0.10755813939272785,"c":1,"h":0.017441860722175129,"w":0.1278195444742839},{"t":"READING PASSAGE 1","x":0.15601503130038169,"y":0.16860465088515608,"c":1,"h":0.015988372635703185,"w":0.28007520039876299},{"x":0.15595733218773716,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1-13, which are based on Reading","y":0.20472430006116016,"w":0.7407154337565105,"h":0.019717773466976118,"c":1},{"w":0.1547788747151693,"c":1,"y":0.22121106306518534,"h":0.019694440685697145,"x":0.15381229026602625,"t":"Passage 1 below."},{"h":0.026202099291033232,"c":1,"x":0.23120300912643083,"y":0.26162790708601869,"t":"Cutty Sark: the fastest sailing ship of all time","w":0.61090225219726557},{"c":1,"h":0.017458706921481104,"t":"The nineteenth century was a period of great technological development in Britain, and for","y":0.30521571180932328,"w":0.70864659627278648,"x":0.15413534838411447},{"w":0.73308268229166673,"t":"shipping the major changes were from wind to steam power, and from wood to iron and steel.","y":0.32260141663901165,"h":0.01751486255672785,"c":1,"x":0.15225565124055787},{"x":0.1541353484593164,"t":"The fastest commercial sailing vessels of all time were clippers, three-masted ships built to","y":0.35755813940185899,"w":0.70864659627278648,"c":1,"h":0.017522350097577633},{"c":1,"w":0.71992482503255206,"x":0.1541353319053248,"t":"transport goods around the world, although some also took passengers. From the 1840s until","y":0.37499999986541432,"h":0.017466194462330997},{"c":1,"t":"1869, when the Suez Canal opened and steam propulsion was replacing sail, clippers dominatec","y":0.39534883726476644,"x":0.15789473789539765,"w":0.74060150146484371,"h":0.013215232033615854},{"t":"world trade. Although many were built, only one has survived more or less intact: Cutty Sark.","x":0.15782765706386367,"y":0.40970576382592849,"w":0.72194478352864577,"h":0.018392960793421009,"c":1},{"y":0.42877906949097744,"c":1,"t":"now on display in Greenwich, southeast London.","x":0.15225564006747969,"w":0.38721804300944007,"h":0.015988372635703185},{"x":0.15413532620267309,"t":"Cutty Sark's unusual name comes from the poem Tam O'Shanter by the Scottish poet Robert","y":0.46361880241570441,"h":0.017485848873258769,"c":1,"w":0.72368423461914055},{"x":0.15407360319705665,"y":0.47950259595151024,"h":0.019825642445128855,"c":1,"t":"Burns. Tam, a farmer, is chased by a witch called Nannie, who is wearing a 'cutty sark' - an","w":0.72004425048828113},{"h":0.017707662588538287,"c":1,"x":0.15225563032317355,"y":0.49839021268625938,"t":"old Scottish name for a short nightdress. The witch is depicted in Cutty Sark's figurehead - the","w":0.73872182210286441},{"c":1,"h":0.017495208145799857,"y":0.51598837179007517,"x":0.1522556473133517,"w":0.73120300292968743,"t":"carving of a woman typically at the front of old sailing ships. In legend, and in Burns's poem,"},{"c":1,"y":0.53477141014256957,"t":"witches cannot cross water, so this was a rather strange choice of name for a ship.","h":0.017707662588538287,"w":0.64097742716471351,"x":0.15225564722444537},{"h":0.017483041275722089,"c":1,"y":0.56976744172966454,"t":"Cutty Sark was built in Dumbarton, Scotland, in 1869, for a shipping company owned by John","x":0.15413533500003665,"w":0.73496241251627603},{"c":1,"y":0.58720930205210398,"h":0.016139056248121397,"x":0.15413533175038993,"t":"Willis. To carry out construction, Willis chose a new shipbuilding firm, Scott & Linton, and","w":0.71992482503255206},{"t":"ensured that the contract with them put him in a very strong position. In the end, the firm was","w":0.72932332356770835,"y":0.60463618819188669,"x":0.15225563135438128,"h":0.017456835189789799,"c":1},{"w":0.51127817789713537,"c":1,"t":"forced out of business, and the ship was finished by a competitor.","h":0.017512990825036434,"y":0.62202189302102984,"x":0.15225564779479844},{"t":"Willis's company was active in the tea trade between China and Britain, where speed could","c":1,"x":0.15413533290404216,"y":0.65697674405504658,"w":0.710526326497396,"h":0.017524221829268827},{"c":1,"y":0.6744186045148467,"x":0.15225564364070057,"h":0.017468066194022192,"w":0.71240600585937497,"t":"bring shipowners both profits and prestige, so Cutty Sark was designed to make the journey"},{"x":0.15225564484220797,"w":0.72180450439453125,"t":"more quickly than any other ship. On her maiden voyage, in 1870, she set sail from London,","y":0.69186046538381152,"h":0.017441859494004786,"c":1},{"y":0.71075581368002783,"w":0.75,"c":1,"h":0.015988372635703185,"t":"carrying large amounts of goods to China. She returned laden with tea, making the journey back","x":0.15225563900874534},{"y":0.7281976742874563,"c":1,"h":0.017455899323944313,"t":"to London in four months. However, Cutty Sark never lived up to the high expectations of her","x":0.152255651251263,"w":0.73308268229166673},{"h":0.018757855132251033,"w":0.74634114583333333,"t":"owner, as a result of bad winds and various misfortunes. On one occasion, in 1872, the ship and","c":1,"x":0.15220878643277294,"y":0.74444844515796571},{"w":0.72007624308268237,"h":0.021643003032657315,"y":0.76129463217113502,"t":"a rival clipper, Thermopylae, left port in China on the same day. Crossing the Indian Ocean,","c":1,"x":0.15216160329162526},{"y":0.7819767439123273,"h":0.015988372635703185,"x":0.15413532830601193,"w":0.7481203206380207,"c":1,"t":"Cutty Sark gained a lead of over 400 miles, but then her rudder was severely damaged in stormy"},{"x":0.1522033071391897,"t":"seas, making her impossible to steer. The ship's crew had the daunting task of repairing the","w":0.71250793457031236,"h":0.019801559251734879,"c":1,"y":0.79715291650522835},{"w":0.75751881917317698,"t":"rudder at sea, and only succeeded at the second attempt. Cutty Sark reached London a week after","c":1,"x":0.15225562791465469,"h":0.017496144011645454,"y":0.8168061816959723},{"y":0.83558057155775545,"x":0.15409341006793265,"w":0.11098611195882163,"c":1,"h":0.016338856851217942,"t":"Thermopylae."},{"h":0.013081395234588733,"c":1,"x":0.15413533835943696,"t":"82","w":0.022556390762329098,"y":0.93023255814215322}]}